# L §5.9: "Choose the table by the read rule (§4.6), treating each version's table as one row ... a new version
# replaces a whole table on its span and never mixes brackets of two years."  v2 (governs 2027..) replaces the
# table: in 2027 its one row [0, inf) gives 9 for every x; in 2026 v1's rows give 1 and 2.
# held (L §8.2): v2's open span read at a law date after the cut 2027-01-01.
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2027-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2027-12-31

source s "s"
band b(n: int): int
input lo: int
input hi: int

version v1
  cite s
  governs 2026..2026
  known 2025-12-01
  b: [0, 10] = 1
  b: (10, inf) = 2

version facts
  cite s
  governs always
  known 2025-12-01
  lo = 5
  hi = 50

version v2
  cite s
  governs 2027..
  known 2026-12-01
  b: [0, inf) = 9

ask q1: b(lo), b(hi) law 2026-06-30
ask q2: b(lo), b(hi) law 2027-06-30
=== expect
q1: (1, 2)
  law date 2026-06-30, cut 2027-01-01
q2: (9, 9)
  held: b row [0, inf) (v2), read for 2027-06-30
  law date 2027-06-30, cut 2027-01-01
